<GuideLayout slug="clinic-doctor-website-checklist">
    <p>
        Patients search on their phones, often worried and in a hurry. A clinic
        website has one job above all others: turn that visit into a booked
        appointment without making the patient hunt for a phone number. This
        checklist covers what to include, based on what we built for
        <a href="/work/heer-heart-lung-clinic/">Heer Heart &amp; Lung Super Speciality Clinic</a>
        in East Delhi.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Say who the doctor is and what they treat</h2>
    <p>
        Put the doctor's name, qualifications and specialities on the first
        screen. If the practice covers more than one speciality, explain why
        that helps the patient. For example, chest symptoms rarely announce
        which organ they come from, so a doctor trained in both cardiology and
        pulmonology can give one clear diagnosis instead of two separate
        opinions.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Make booking one tap away</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>A visible appointment button on every page, especially on mobile.</li>
        <li>A WhatsApp enquiry option, since many patients prefer messaging to calling.</li>
        <li>Clear clinic hours, address and a map link.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Write pages for symptoms and services</h2>
    <p>
        People search for symptoms and treatments, not clinic names. Give each
        important condition or service its own page with plain-language
        explanations, so your clinic can appear when someone searches for what
        they are experiencing. Keep claims factual, and check your medical
        council's rules on advertising before publishing.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Show real reviews</h2>
    <p>
        Reviews are the strongest trust signal for a clinic. Embedding your
        live Google reviews on the site means new reviews appear without any
        extra work, and they match what patients see on Google Maps.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Be fast and easy on phones</h2>
    <p>
        Most patients arrive from a phone search, so the site must load
        quickly and be readable without zooming. A lightweight, mobile-first
        build is better than a heavy design.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Set up local search properly</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            A complete <a href="/guides/google-business-profile-setup-checklist/">Google Business Profile</a>
            with the right categories, hours and photos.
        </li>
        <li>The same name, address and phone number on the website and the profile.</li>
        <li>Page titles, descriptions and structured data so search engines understand the clinic.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>7. Protect patient privacy</h2>
    <p>
        Do not ask for medical details through a simple contact form. Keep
        enquiry forms to name, phone and a short message, and tell patients how
        their details are used.
    </p>

    <h2>Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Doctor profile and specialities above the fold</li>
        <li>Appointment button and WhatsApp enquiry on every page</li>
        <li>Symptom and service pages</li>
        <li>Live Google review embed</li>
        <li>Fast mobile experience</li>
        <li>Google Business Profile aligned with the website</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        See how this came together in the
        <a href="/work/heer-heart-lung-clinic/">Heer Heart &amp; Lung Clinic case study</a>,
        or read about our
        <a href="/services/web-design-for-clinics-doctors/">website design for clinics and doctors</a>.
        Clinics in Delhi can also see our
        <a href="/locations/delhi/">Delhi page</a>.
    </p>
</GuideLayout>
